	"_comment": "Slovenščina (Slovenian). Composed shape is Subject + passive participle with elided copula (je), exactly how a Slovenian status label reads: 'Datoteka izbrisana' not 'Datoteka je izbrisana'. No articles (article.none). Masculine passive participles are authored bare (izbrisan, poslan, odprt — no vowel ending), so agreement is a pure suffix-add with no strip: 'a' (f) / 'o' (n) from the masculine base, verified against all 19 verbs including the -t class (odprt → odprta/odprto). The 'gerund' slot carries the verbal-noun progress register real Slovenian UIs show (brisanje, pisanje, branje); 'start' and 'run' use a plain deverbative-adjacent noun (začetek, zaganjanje) rather than force an awkward derivation. 'push' and 'commit' have no inherited Slovenian verb; pushati/komitati are informal developer-register loanword coinages on the productive -ati class (flagged for honesty — attested dev-slang currency, not neutral standard usage; same borrowing pattern as this codebase's own de.json 'committen'). otrok → otroci is a genuine irregular/palatalised plural (flagged, same treatment as German Kind → Kinder elsewhere in this repo); note child is MASCULINE in Slovenian (otrok) where the Slovak cognate set is neuter (dieťa) — a real cross-language gender difference, not an authoring slip. NOMINATIVE-only composition, accepted deliberately: real Slovenian also has a DUAL number (unused here — the engine's noun table has no dual slot, so exactly-two counts fall back to the plain plural) and alternates case after numerals/prepositions/verbs; this engine has no case or dual slot, so 'other' is always the plain nominative plural and the fail-sentence object stays nominative rather than the accusative a native speaker would inflect to.",
